7. MANUAL PAYMENT SYSTEM — IMPORTANT BUSINESS REQUIREMENT

This platform uses manual transfers. Do not build this around mandatory payment-gateway integration.

A. Store owner's payment-method management

Each business owner must be able to add, edit, activate, deactivate, reorder, and remove their payment methods safely.

A payment method can contain fields such as:

- Payment method name.
- Bank or mobile-money provider.
- Account holder name.
- Account number or wallet number.
- Branch or other required transfer details.
- Currency.
- Transfer instructions.
- Optional QR code or payment image.
- Whether the method is currently active.

The owner must be able to add multiple methods.

Validate the fields and display only active methods to customers.

Do not expose private configuration or administrative data through public APIs. Public storefronts should display only the payment instructions intentionally made public by the owner.

B. Customer checkout

When customers place an order:

1. Show the order summary.
2. Show the correct total, currency, and item quantities.
3. Show the available payment methods belonging to that store.
4. Let the customer select one method.
5. Display the account holder and account details clearly.
6. Provide copy buttons for account numbers and other relevant transfer details.
7. Explain how much the customer needs to transfer.
8. Create the order with a clear pending-payment status.
9. Store a snapshot of the selected payment instructions so later edits to the owner's payment method do not silently alter an existing order's payment details.

Do not display a successful payment message merely because the customer opened checkout or selected a method.

C. Customer reports that payment was made

After transferring money outside Telebiz, the customer must be able to select:

"I have paid" / "Submit payment"

The customer should then be able to provide:

- Transaction/reference number.
- Optional payment screenshot or receipt, where supported.
- Additional note, if needed.

Validate uploads and references. Protect uploaded receipts from unauthorized access.

Submitting payment must change the order to a state such as "payment_submitted" or "awaiting_verification".

It must NOT:

- Automatically mark the order as paid.
- Automatically verify a bank transfer.
- Automatically release goods or services.
- Give the customer authority to approve their own payment.

Prevent duplicate submissions and repeated requests from creating duplicate payment records.

D. Store owner's payment-verification dashboard

Give business owners a clear payment-verification queue.

For each submitted payment, show:

- Order number.
- Customer information needed to process the order.
- Amount and currency.
- Selected payment method.
- Account details that were shown at checkout.
- Transaction/reference number.
- Uploaded receipt, if provided.
- Submission time.
- Current verification status.

The owner or an employee with the specific payment-verification permission must be able to review the payment outside Telebiz against the money actually received.

They can then:

- Confirm payment received.
- Reject the submission.
- Request more information.

When rejecting, require a reason and show that reason to the customer.

Only an authorized owner or employee can confirm a manual transfer.

E. Payment status and order status must be separate and consistent

Implement a documented state machine. At minimum, support the appropriate equivalents of:

Payment:

- "pending"
- "payment_submitted"
- "verified"
- "rejected"
- "refunded", only when a refund has actually been confirmed and recorded

Order:

- "pending_payment"
- "awaiting_verification"
- "confirmed"
- "processing"
- "ready_for_pickup" or "out_for_delivery", where applicable
- "completed" or "delivered"
- "cancelled"

Use only valid transitions. An order cannot be delivered simply because the customer submitted a receipt.

When an owner confirms payment:

- Verify the owner or employee's permission.
- Persist the verification event.
- Record who verified it and when.
- Update the payment and related order consistently.
- Notify the customer.
- Allow the owner to begin order fulfillment.

When a payment is rejected:

- Record who rejected it and why.
- Notify the customer.
- Allow the customer to correct and resubmit where appropriate.

All related database updates must be transactional and safe against duplicate requests.

F. Store owner fulfills the order

After verified payment, the owner must be able to:

- Confirm the order.
- Mark it as processing.
- Mark it ready for pickup, where applicable.
- Mark it out for delivery, where applicable.
- Mark it delivered or completed.
- Cancel it with a reason where permitted.

The customer must see the real order progress.

Only users with the appropriate permissions may perform these actions.

For cash-on-delivery or other special payment arrangements, support them only when the store owner explicitly configures them. They must have their own clear payment-confirmation rules.

